condition_name: Foreign Bodies in the Airways
condition_slug: foreign-bodies-in-the-airways
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: Children (most commonly) may aspirate pieces of play objects or food items accidentally into the airway. This may present as serious emergency with imminent asphyxia.
clinical_features[6]:
  - type: Laryngeal Level
    features[1]: Acute upper respiratory tract obstruction with difficulty in breathing and stridor may occur immediately or progressively.
  - type: Post-aspiration
    features[1]: "Initial dyspnoea and cough may subside if the object passes down, but symptoms may return later."
  - type: Sharp Objects
    features[1]: Fish bone or pins may be impacted on the vocal cord leading to oedema and progressive obstruction.
  - type: Severe Cases
    features[1]: "Stridor, severe cyanosis and imminent asphyxia requiring immediate intervention to prevent a fatal outcome may occur."
  - type: Tracheal/Bronchial
    features[3]: Small objects such as seeds may transverse the larynx and become arrested in the trachea or bronchus.,Vegetables such as peanuts may cause inflammatory reactions such as pneumonitis.,Two-way stridor often occurs with tracheal foreign bodies.
  - type: Lower Airways
    features[1]: "In the lower airways objects may remain for long periods, with unexplained chest symptoms."
investigations[1]: Radiograph of neck and chest
treatment:
  goals[2]: To maintain the airway and adequate respiratory function,Remove the foreign object as expeditiously as possible
  non_drug[2]: Immediate removal under anaesthesia by direct laryngoscopy or bronchoscopy as appropriate.,Tracheostomy where necessary to maintain airway
  drug[2]: "Antibiotic prophylaxis if necessary (for 3 days): - Amoxicillin: - Child: 6-12 years: 250 mg orally 12 hourly - under 6 years: 125 mg orally 12 hourly","Steroids - Hydrocortisone (for pneumonitis): - Child 1 month - 1 year: initially 25 mg by intravenous or intramuscular injection q8hr; - 1 - 6 years: initially 50 mg q8hr; - 6 - 12 years: initially 100 mg q8hr; - 12 - 18 years: initially 100 - 500 mg q8hr, - adjusted in all age groups according to response"
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[0]:
  supportive_measures[2]: Oxygen,Steam inhalation/nebulizer
differential_diagnoses[4]: Acute laryngitis,Acute laryngeal oedema,Bronchopneumonia,Pulmonary tuberculosis
complications[2]: Life-threatening asphyxia,Lung collapse and atelectasis
prevention[1]: Vigilant supervision of young children
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null